---
const title = 'Cotonou + Ouidah, Benin';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
  <meta name="description" content="Travel notes from Cotonou and Ouidah, Benin." />
  <title>{title}</title>
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
  <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Mono:wght@400;500&family=DM+Sans:wght@300;400;500;600&display=swap" rel="stylesheet" />
</head>
<body>
  <main class="page">
    <header>
      <p class="kicker">Travel notes</p>
      <h1>Cotonou + Ouidah, Benin</h1>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2>Hotel</h2>
      <p>Stayed at Sofitel Cotonou Marina Hotel &amp; Spa in a sea view room with breakfast.</p>
      <p>Paid <strong>1,057,444.00 XOF</strong>, equivalent to <strong>$1,837</strong>.</p>
      <p>One thing to know is that even if you book online and put in your card, they may not actually charge it. I still had to pay in person at the hotel.</p>
      <p>The hotel has a lot going on so you can easily spend a full day there without really leaving.</p>
      <p>There are multiple restaurants, bars, a spa, indoor and outdoor pools, cinema, club/lounge and a few other things. The ground floor bar also gets pretty lively at night.</p>
      <p>You do not have to be staying at the hotel to use everything either. They take external reservations for the restaurants, club and other facilities.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Getting there from Lagos</h2>
      <p>I used <strong>Benify</strong> and paid <strong>₦450,000</strong> for a GX460 from Lagos to Cotonou.</p>
      <p>Also paid <strong>₦10,000</strong> for stamps and border assistance.</p>
      <p>They had someone waiting at the border to help with the stamps, changing money and the general process.</p>
      <p>I changed about <strong>₦580,000</strong> and got roughly <strong>255,000 XOF</strong>.</p>
      <p>I also got a local SIM and data for around <strong>7,000 XOF</strong>.</p>
      <p>Having cash is useful, especially once you leave the hotel.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Day 1: Ouidah</h2>
      <p>Booked a driver for the day for <strong>₦105,000</strong>.</p>
      <p>Pickup was from Sofitel at around <strong>11:30 AM</strong>.</p>
      <p>We did:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Point of No Return</li>
        <li>Fondation Zinsou</li>
        <li>Python Temple</li>
        <li>Ouidah historical areas</li>
        <li>Ouidah Sur Mer for lunch and the beach</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The original plan had Casa Del Papa but I swapped that for Ouidah Sur Mer.</p>
      <p>That was enough for the day. I would not really try to cram much more into it.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Day 2: Hotel day</h2>
      <p>I mostly stayed around Sofitel and used the facilities.</p>

      <h3>Spa</h3>
      <p>I would budget around <strong>30,000 to 70,000 XOF</strong>, depending on the treatment.</p>
      <p>My room came with <strong>30 minutes complimentary sauna</strong>.</p>
      <p>You also get access to the indoor pool once you pay for the spa.</p>

      <h3>Cinema</h3>
      <p>Cinema was around <strong>15,000 XOF</strong>.</p>

      <h3>Sunset drinks at Corniche</h3>
      <p>We spent around <strong>45,000 XOF</strong> for:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>4 shots</li>
        <li>2 cocktails</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Day 3: Cotonou</h2>
      <p>Used the same driver arrangement.</p>
      <p><strong>₦105,000 for the day</strong>, pickup around <strong>11:30 AM</strong> from Sofitel.</p>
      <p>The plan was:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Amazone Esplanade</li>
        <li>Port graffiti wall</li>
        <li>Bio Guera Monument</li>
        <li>Art Market</li>
        <li>Abandoned plane</li>
        <li>Corniche</li>
      </ul>
      <p>We also added a few things on the day:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Super U</li>
        <li>Quad biking at Family Beach</li>
        <li>A local Nigerian restaurant</li>
      </ul>
      <p>None of those were planned beforehand.</p>
      <p>I would definitely leave some room in the day instead of planning every single stop because it is easy to add things once you are out.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Byblos</h2>
      <p>Planning to do this.</p>
      <p>It is an upscale club, so this one really depends on how much you want to spend.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Food</h2>
      <p>Room service can be cheap or expensive depending on what you order.</p>
      <p>Rough prices:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Nuggets and fries: around <strong>6,000 XOF</strong></li>
        <li>Penne pasta: around <strong>8,000 XOF</strong></li>
        <li>Bolognese: around <strong>12,000 XOF</strong></li>
        <li>Burger: around <strong>24,000 XOF</strong></li>
        <li>Steak: around <strong>32,000 XOF</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p>For a normal meal I would say budget around <strong>15,000 to 25,000 XOF per person</strong>.</p>
      <p>If you want 3 courses and drinks, then more like <strong>30,000 to 40,000 XOF+</strong>.</p>
      <p>There are plenty of places to eat in the hotel too so you are not stuck with room service.</p>
      <p>L’Ami is one of the nicer restaurants and there are a few other options depending on what you want.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Getting around</h2>
      <p>For proper sightseeing days I used a private driver at <strong>₦105,000 per day</strong>.</p>
      <p>For smaller trips around Cotonou you can just use taxis or Yango and spend much less.</p>
      <p>For Ouidah though, having one driver for the full day was worth it.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Language</h2>
      <p>French helps a lot.</p>
      <p>Reception and concierge usually have at least one person who speaks English, but not everyone does and some things can still get lost in translation.</p>
      <p>Once you are out in town, English is much less reliable.</p>
      <p>Google Translate helps a lot.</p>
      <p>I also met a guy called Femi at the hotel who spoke Yoruba, which made things easier for a bit.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Hotel atmosphere</h2>
      <p>The hotel is very classy and pretty calm.</p>
      <p>Even though outsiders can book the restaurants, bars and club, it still feels properly five star.</p>
      <p>People are generally composed and respectful. No loud shouting or random chaos.</p>
      <p>I liked that part a lot.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Paying for things</h2>
      <p>On the hotel grounds, tap to pay worked fine for me.</p>
      <p>I used <strong>both</strong> my Pixel and iPhone 17 to pay.</p>
      <p>My GTBank card did not work, although to be fair I have basically never been able to get that card to work properly abroad anyway.</p>
      <p>There are also <strong>2 ATMs</strong> at the hotel if you need cash.</p>
      <p>Once you leave the hotel grounds, I would assume cash first.</p>
      <p>Tap to pay was not really a thing from my experience and a lot of places are much more cash or mobile money based.</p>
      <p>At Ouidah Sur Mer, they actually asked if I could pay by <strong>MoMo</strong> before I eventually paid cash.</p>
      <p>So I would definitely carry enough CFA whenever you are going into town or doing an Ouidah day.</p>
      <p>Cash is useful for:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>attractions</li>
        <li>tips</li>
        <li>drivers</li>
        <li>smaller restaurants</li>
        <li>markets</li>
        <li>beach activities</li>
        <li>random stops</li>
      </ul>
      <p>At the hotel, phone payments and card terminals are much easier.</p>
    </section>
  </main>
</body>
</html>

